Below is a ready‑to‑paste JavaScript snippet that shows how to create a new `ChartSeries` with your supplied data and immediately attach it to the desired chart layout.  
The `ChartSeriesGenerator` helper expects **stringified** JavaScript arrays/objects, so we use `JSON.stringify()` to convert the raw values before passing them in.

```js
// 1️⃣  Your raw data (replace these with your real values)
const axisLabelsArray   = ['January', 'February', 'March'];   // <-- axis labels
const dataPointsArray   = [120, 150, 90];                    // <-- data points
const defaultColor      = '#1f77b4';                         // <-- default color
const chartLayoutObject = {
  title: 'Monthly Sales',
  xAxis: { title: 'Month' },
  yAxis: { title: 'Sales (units)' },
  // … any other layout options you need
};

// 2️⃣  Call the generator – note the JSON.stringify() wrappers
const chartSeries = ChartSeriesGenerator({
  labels: JSON.stringify(axisLabelsArray),   // stringified array of labels
  data:   JSON.stringify(dataPointsArray),   // stringified array of data points
  color:  defaultColor,                     // optional – defaults to a preset if omitted
  chartLayout: JSON.stringify(chartLayoutObject) // stringified layout object
});

// 3️⃣  The generator already attaches the series to the layout,
//     but if you need to keep a reference you can do:
//     chartLayoutObject.series.push(chartSeries);

// 4️⃣  Render / update your chart (depends on your charting library)
//     e.g., chart.update();  // or whatever method your library exposes
```

### Why `JSON.stringify()`?

`ChartSeriesGenerator` is defined to accept *string* representations of the arrays/objects.  
By stringifying the raw JavaScript values we satisfy that contract while keeping the code readable.

### Quick checklist

| Step | What to do | Where to put it |
|------|------------|-----------------|
| 1️⃣ | Define your raw data (`axisLabelsArray`, `dataPointsArray`, `defaultColor`, `chartLayoutObject`). | Anywhere before the call – usually at the top of your module or component. |
| 2️⃣ | Call `ChartSeriesGenerator` with the stringified arguments. | Right after defining the data. |
| 3️⃣ | (Optional) Keep a reference to the returned series if you need to manipulate it later. | Immediately after the call. |
| 4️⃣ | Trigger a chart render/update. | Depends on your charting library (e.g., `chart.update()`). |

Feel free to replace the placeholder arrays and layout object with your actual data. Once you run this snippet, the new series will be attached to `chartLayoutObject` automatically, and you can proceed to render or update the chart as usual.

